---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Grade from '../components/Grade.astro';
import State from '../components/State.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import { ped } from '../data/timelines';
---
<Base
  title="Program for Evolutionary Dynamics"
  description="Timeline of Jeffrey Epstein’s funding of and access to Harvard’s Program for Evolutionary Dynamics, and the University’s response, sourced mainly to Harvard’s own 2020 review."
  eyebrow="Harvard · Faculty of Arts and Sciences"
  lede="Epstein’s $6.5 million gift founded this research program in 2003. Harvard took no money from him after 2008, yet he kept a key card and the use of an office, and visited more than 40 times before 2018. Harvard closed the program in 2021."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  basis="Harvard’s 2020 General Counsel report"
>
  <ul class="figures full">
    <li><span class="num">$6.5M</span><span class="lbl">Founding gift, 2003<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">40+</span><span class="lbl">Visits to the Program’s offices, 2010 to 2018<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">$7.5M</span><span class="lbl">From introduced donors for Professor Nowak’s work, 2010 to 2015<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">2021</span><span class="lbl">Program closed by the Faculty of Arts and Sciences<Cite id="crimson-2021-nowak" /></span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
  <p>
    The Program for Evolutionary Dynamics was a mathematical biology group led by Professor Martin Nowak. It was
    established with a $6.5 million gift from Epstein in 2003 and worked from a private office building at One Brattle
    Square in Harvard Square.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" />
  </p>
  <p>
    Harvard’s review counted $9.1 million in gifts from Epstein to the whole University between 1998 and 2008. After
    his 2008 conviction, President Drew Faust decided that Harvard would accept nothing further from him, and the review
    found that it did not.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /><Cite id="hm-2020-report" /> The Program is where the
    relationship continued in other forms: physical access, a presence on the Program’s website, and gifts from donors
    he had introduced.
  </p>

  <Callout title="A figure that is often misread">
    <p>
      The $9.1 million is the total of Epstein’s gifts to Harvard, all received by 2008. It is not the amount given to
      this Program, and none of it arrived after the conviction. Some coverage gives the total as $9,179,000 or
      “nearly $9.2 million”; the difference is rounding.<Cite id="crimson-2020-report" />
      The money that reached Professor Nowak’s work after 2008 came from other donors.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="timeline">Timeline: funding, access and response</h2>
  <Timeline events={ped} id="ped" label="Program for Evolutionary Dynamics timeline" />

  <h2 id="access">What the access consisted of</h2>
  <p>Harvard’s review describes four things.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Visits.</strong> The records are imprecise. The review understood that he came to the Program’s offices more than 40 times between 2010 and 2018.</li>
    <li><strong>Entry.</strong> He had a visitor key card, the passcode, and a key to an individual office.</li>
    <li><strong>An office.</strong> The Program informally called one room “Jeffrey’s Office.” Professor Nowak told reviewers that other visitors used it too.</li>
    <li><strong>A web presence.</strong> The Program’s site linked to his foundation’s websites from 2013 and carried a page about him in 2014, until a complaint led to its removal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The review said he used the visits mainly to meet prominent academics from the Cambridge area. It reported that it
    “did not find evidence” that he engaged with undergraduate students during the visits or as a Visiting
    Fellow.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /> That sentence is limited to undergraduates and to what the review could find.
    The Crimson’s account of the report notes that he attended one of Professor Nowak’s undergraduate
    classes.<Cite id="crimson-2020-report" />
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Volume of contact</h2>
  <p>
    After the 2026 Justice Department release, reporters searching the public database found Professor Nowak’s name in
    more than 4,000 results.<Cite id="ihe-2026-names" /> Outlets describe the unit differently (documents, results,
    appearances), and search hits include duplicates and forwarded threads. The number shows that contact was
    frequent. It is not a count of messages, and it says nothing by itself about their content.
  </p>

  <h2 id="response">Institutional response</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>What Harvard did, and when</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Action</th><th>Status</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>2008</th><td data-label="Action">No further gifts accepted from Epstein</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>2010 to 2018</th><td data-label="Action">Physical access to the Program continued; the review found no evidence that University leadership knew of the website page</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>May 2020</th><td data-label="Action">Report published; Professor Nowak placed on paid leave; unspent $200,937 redirected to survivor organisations</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="bacow-2020" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>March 2021</th><td data-label="Action">Policy violations found; two-year restrictions; Program closed</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A2" /><Cite id="crimson-2021-nowak" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>March 2023</th><td data-label="Action">Restrictions lifted</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A2" /><Cite id="crimson-2023-nowak" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>February 2026</th><td data-label="Action">Second paid leave pending a formal investigation; outcome not published</td><td data-label="Status"><State s="activity" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A2" /><Cite id="crimson-2026-nowak-leave" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">What the record does not show</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No genetic or DNA-related activity is tied to the Program in any source reviewed here.</li>
    <li>The 2021 findings concerned professional conduct, campus access and endorsement policies. We found no published finding, either way, on research misconduct.</li>
    <li>Professor Nowak is not accused of a crime. He has said he did not know of Epstein’s crimes and regrets the connection.<Cite id="hm-2026-nowak" /></li>
    <li>The outcome of the investigation opened in 2026 was not public when this page was last reviewed.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
